\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Second and third derivatives}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{A test on the characteristic polynomial can put a conjugate
pair on the imaginary axis. It does not give the sign of
#{\ell_{1}}. The formulas in \ref{fnip-0099} use second and
third derivatives. \citet{pp. 65–66}{ermentrout2010mathematical}
sketches the Routh–Hurwitz test in Troy's existence argument
for the Hodgkin–Huxley equations, and records that a rigorous
account of which side carries the periodic orbit remains open.
The signs in the examples are a numerical evaluation of the
invariant formula. In dimension greater than two, quadratic
terms on the [center manifold](fnip-0059) enter that
coefficient. \citet{p. 153}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear} records
that projecting onto the eigenspace of #{\pm i\omega} leaves
those quadratic terms out.}
